# scenarios/s24/templates/webhook.yml.j2
# The S24 fault, in one object.
#
# The ONLY thing that differs between the two halves of the scenario is
# `failurePolicy` — rendered from s24_failure_policy, which the playbook
# loops over as ["Fail", "Ignore"]. Endpoint, timeout, rules, selectors and
# the object name are byte-identical in both halves, so any difference the
# run measures is attributable to that single field and nothing else.
#
# ── Why this cannot wedge the control plane (or its own removal) ────────────
# A `failurePolicy: Fail` webhook is a live footgun: if it intercepts a
# resource the control plane itself must write, every controller that writes
# that resource stalls, and if it intercepts admissionregistration.k8s.io it
# can block the very API call that would remove it. Four independent guards
# keep this one inert outside its probe:
#
#   1. operations: ["CREATE"] only. DELETE and UPDATE are never intercepted,
#      so `kubectl delete validatingwebhookconfiguration` — and the deletion
#      of every object inside the probe namespace — is untouched by
#      construction. The removal path can never be admission-blocked.
#   2. apiGroups: [""] / resources: [configmaps, pods] only. The
#      admissionregistration.k8s.io group is not in the rule set, so this
#      webhook cannot intercept the creation or replacement of itself or of
#      any other webhook configuration. Nor can it touch nodes, leases,
#      endpointslices or events — the objects kubelet heartbeats, leader
#      election and endpoint publication depend on.
#   3. namespaceSelector. Only a namespace carrying the label
#      `{{ s24_probe_label_key }}: "true"` is in scope, and the scenario puts that
#      label on exactly one namespace it created ({{ s24_probe_ns }}) and
#      deletes. kube-system, kube-public and default are all out of scope, so
#      no system component's CREATE traffic passes through the black hole.
#   4. objectSelector. Within that namespace, only objects that themselves
#      carry the same label are intercepted. This matters concretely: the
#      root-CA publisher controller CREATEs a `kube-root-ca.crt` ConfigMap in
#      every new namespace, and with `Fail` and no objectSelector that
#      controller would hot-loop against the black hole for the life of the
#      fault. It carries no probe label, so it is never intercepted.
#
# Guards 1 and 2 alone are sufficient for the removal path; 3 and 4 keep the
# blast radius down to the objects the scenario deliberately creates.
#
# ── Why the endpoint black-holes rather than refuses ────────────────────────
# clientConfig.url points at {{ s24_blackhole_url }}. Two things make that a
# black hole, and only the second one actually does the work.
#
#   (a) The address is unassigned. It sits on the cluster's pod-network
#       segment (192.168.200.0/24; terraform/main.tf: "the pod network is
#       used by this cluster alone", VMs occupy .49-.61 only), so no listener
#       exists to send an RST and no host will ever claim it. This is defence
#       in depth: if the DROP below somehow failed to install, the webhook
#       still cannot reach a real service.
#
#   (b) The scenario installs an explicit `iptables -I OUTPUT -d <ip> -p tcp
#       --dport <port> -j DROP` on every control-plane node before the
#       webhook is registered, and removes it in the playbook's `always`
#       block. THIS is what produces a timeout.
#
# (a) alone is NOT sufficient, and the first version of this scenario was
# wrong about that. Measured on the live cluster from cp1 and cp2 — the exact
# hosts and network namespace the API server dials from, since kube-apiserver
# is a hostNetwork static pod:
#
#     curl exit=7 (CURLE_COULDNT_CONNECT) after 3071 ms   from cp2
#     curl exit=7 (CURLE_COULDNT_CONNECT) after 3095 ms   from cp1
#
# not exit=28 (CURLE_OPERATION_TIMEDOUT). The address is on a directly
# connected /24 with no router on the segment, so the kernel has to resolve
# it by ARP; nothing answers, neighbour resolution gives up after ~3 s and
# connect() returns EHOSTUNREACH. The call fails fast with a *connection
# error* and `timeoutSeconds` is never reached. That is a different fault
# from the one this scenario is named for, and it would have made the
# recorded latencies mean something other than what the header claimed.
#
# An `ip route add blackhole <ip>/32` does not fix it either, and is worse:
# for locally generated traffic the kernel's fib_props table maps
# RTN_BLACKHOLE to -EINVAL (RTN_UNREACHABLE to -EHOSTUNREACH, RTN_PROHIBIT to
# -EACCES), so connect() fails *immediately* rather than after the ARP
# timeout. A blackhole route silently discards packets it is *forwarding*;
# it errors out packets the host itself originates.
#
# A netfilter DROP is different because of where it sits in the transmit
# path: ip_local_out() runs the OUTPUT hook BEFORE ip_finish_output2() does
# neighbour resolution. The SYN is discarded before ARP is ever attempted, no
# error is returned to the socket, and TCP simply retransmits until something
# above it gives up. The something above it is the API server's own admission
# deadline — i.e. exactly `timeoutSeconds` below. The playbook asserts this at
# inject time (curl must exit 28, not 7) so the mechanism can never silently
# regress to (a) again.
#
# This is also why the endpoint is not a Service with no ready backends:
# kube-proxy installs an explicit REJECT rule for a ClusterIP with no
# endpoints, which comes back as "connection refused" in milliseconds — the
# same class of failure as the ARP case, and equally not a timeout.
apiVersion: admissionregistration.k8s.io/v1
kind: ValidatingWebhookConfiguration
metadata:
  name: {{ s24_webhook_name }}
  labels:
    scenario: s24
    # Teardown deletes by this label as well as by name, so a webhook left by
    # a partially-failed run is still removed even if the name ever changes.
    fault-inject: "s24"
webhooks:
  - name: blackhole.s24.failure-semantics.local
    admissionReviewVersions: ["v1"]
    sideEffects: "None"
    matchPolicy: Equivalent
    # ── the one field under test ──
    failurePolicy: {{ s24_failure_policy }}
    # Explicit, and short enough that a rejected request returns well inside
    # kubectl's --request-timeout. Note that BOTH halves pay this latency:
    # the API server waits the full timeout either way and only then consults
    # failurePolicy to decide whether to reject or continue. The scenario
    # records the latency of both so that cost is visible, not assumed.
    timeoutSeconds: {{ s24_webhook_timeout }}
    clientConfig:
      # No caBundle: with a url-based clientConfig the API server falls back
      # to the host trust roots. Irrelevant here — the TCP connection never
      # completes, so TLS is never negotiated.
      url: "{{ s24_blackhole_url }}"
    namespaceSelector:
      matchLabels:
        {{ s24_probe_label_key }}: "true"
    objectSelector:
      matchLabels:
        {{ s24_probe_label_key }}: "true"
    rules:
      - apiGroups: [""]
        apiVersions: ["v1"]
        operations: ["CREATE"]
        resources: ["configmaps", "pods"]
        scope: "Namespaced"
